codeExercise.solution:
const net = { rtt: 100, kbPerMs: 1 };
const monolith = { main: { kb: 300, imports: [] } };
const chain = { main: { kb: 100, imports: ['checkout'] }, checkout: { kb: 100, imports: ['payment'] }, payment: { kb: 100, imports: [] } };
const fanOut = { main: { kb: 100, imports: ['checkout', 'payment'] }, checkout: { kb: 100, imports: [] }, payment: { kb: 100, imports: [] } };
function routeReadyTime(graph, root, { rtt, kbPerMs, preloaded = [] }) {
  if (!graph[root]) return 0;
  const reachable = new Set([root]);
  const importers = new Map();
  const stack = [root];
  while (stack.length) {
    const name = stack.pop();
    for (const dep of graph[name].imports) {
      if (!importers.has(dep)) importers.set(dep, []);
      importers.get(dep).push(name);
      if (!reachable.has(dep)) { reachable.add(dep); stack.push(dep); }
    }
  }
  const memo = new Map();
  const finish = name => {
    if (memo.has(name)) return memo.get(name);
    const start = name === root || preloaded.includes(name) ? 0 : Math.min(...importers.get(name).map(finish));
    const done = start + rtt + graph[name].kb / kbPerMs;
    memo.set(name, done);
    return done;
  };
  return Math.max(...[...reachable].map(finish));
}

codeExercise.solutionNote:
**The plausible wrong answer (believing splitting is free):**

```js
function routeReadyTime(graph, root, { rtt, kbPerMs }) {
  if (!graph[root]) return 0;
  const largest = Math.max(...Object.values(graph).map(c => c.kb)); // "smaller chunks = faster; the biggest chunk decides"
  return rtt + largest / kbPerMs;
}
```

This reveals **web-performance-optimization.m3**. The starter's own placeholder — one RTT plus total bytes over bandwidth, ignoring the import graph's shape entirely — predicts 400 ms for the three-level `chain` fixture, which actually takes 600 ms. This second version keeps the same belief in a different shape: it assumes only the single biggest chunk decides the timing, when in fact a chain of dynamic imports each adds its own round trip that the biggest-chunk-alone model never charges for.

