codeExercise.solutionNote:
**The plausible wrong answer:**

```js
function prioritizeFindings(findings) {
  const effort = { S: 0, M: 1, L: 2 };
  return [...findings].sort((a, b) => effort[a.effort] - effort[b.effort] || b.pages - a.pages).map(f => f.id); // "knock out the easy ones"
}
```

It puts `dup-ids` first and the booking-blocking trap near the end. It reveals **`accessibility-auditing.m3`**: treating all violations as equally urgent (here, ranking by convenience).

